Headless apps give the main window's size before scripts load

THeadlessSession takes the main window's size, setMainWindowSize() in pixels or setScreenSize() in characters,
only once a profile has loaded. Every script that runs as the profile loads reads the default of 90 by 25
characters instead, and a package that fits its windows to the main window then, as the starter UI fits its dock,
lays them out for a size the app never shows. A console with autoWrap keeps the wrap each line had as it arrived,
so a line echoed then stays wrapped at that width after the window grows.

- THeadlessSession::Options takes mainWindowSize (pixels) or else screenSize (characters). The area is laid out
  with the font and borders the save left before the first script runs, which reads that size and is sent no
  sysWindowResizeEvent for it. The same size given again after the load changes nothing.
- A screen size of more than 65535 either way is refused, as setScreenSize() refuses it. A main window that
  holds no character of the profile's font is not taken, and setMainWindowSize() refuses it once loaded.
- The GUI and the headless runner are unchanged.

Tested by HeadlessSessionTest (test_aMainWindowGivenAtStartIsWhatTheFirstScriptReads,
test_aScreenSizeGivenAtStartIsWhatTheFirstScriptReads) and by Mudlet/android's native/tests/test_mudlet_engine.py.

Carried by Mudlet/android's scripts/apply-mudlet-patches.sh until Mudlet/Mudlet has it.
Upstream: not opened yet

